  "live_records": {
    "q-53028": {
      "dom_id": "step-q53028",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191822/assets/8bd85835b1bb951df5773824b45f53527a4cda3c4e988f7b30f151e289d47459.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, we know that \n$m∠A=m∠B.$\nWhat is the measure of the angle $A?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $x$ be the measure of the interior angle at $A.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm∠A=m∠B=x.\n$$\n\n*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $C.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{110}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-{110}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={70}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+x+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={110}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={55}^{∘}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m∠A+m∠B+m∠C={180}^{∘}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠C}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at }C}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠B \\\\ {110}^{∘} & =x+x \\\\ {110}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ {55}^{∘} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
