    "q-77566": {
      "dom_id": "step-q77566",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider a triangle $△ABC$, where an exterior angle at $A$ is ${119}^{∘}$ and an exterior angle at $C$ is ${103}^{∘}.$ What is the measure of the interior angle $B?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a corresponding picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191822/assets/6fc411e7715044257a6818fe63fb3b7bd212c22a2f3d279f282b2b7161be3ecc.png)\n\n   \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at $B$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{119}^{∘}+{103}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {222}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={138}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{138}^{∘} \\\\  & ={42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠B={42}^{∘}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
